ssembly  released their most ambitious total war  to date it was a lot of firsts it was  the first of a trilogy it was the first  total war to branch out of the history  books the first total war to be attached  to another major ip and the first hot  award to feature big daft dragons  zombies magic and gross demons while the  factions in the history-based total war  games shared some similarities each race  in warhammer is so distinct and unique  the developers and diners had to tap  into decades of law and models to ensure  they do their in-game depictions justice  same with the campaign map and the wider  world adaptations would have to be made  for the sake of gameplay while also  keeping true to the source material to  keep fans of both franchises happy  characters in generals were made to  stand out more by giving them unique  spells and abilities similar to how  they'd been treated in the tabletop game  the attention to detail was incredible  and having seen creative assemblies  warhammer room and the hundreds of books  used to tap into the law it's plain to  see that they've done their due  diligence while it released with  relatively few factions in comparison to  other total war games only four in the  core game at launch the races were so  different that it meant subsequent  playthroughs made for a much more  different experience than in any other  previous game consolidating a kingdom as  the empire was way different than wiring  your way across the old world as the  orcs being a new total war in a brand  new direction and being attached to a  much beloved ip the excitement was  electric for total warhammer launched  with its share of controversy  the warriors of chaos are one of the  most popular and prominent factions in  the warhammer franchise which may have  been a factor when it came to how the  community reacted to the announcement  that they'd be available as dlc they  were made available to buy at launch but  free for players who pre-ordered the  game and this decision was widely  criticized by fans of both total war and  warhammer a reaction that creative  assembly was disheartened by in an  attempt to remedy this they made the  faction free dlc for anyone who  purchased the game the week of its  release another case of a hiccup shaping  the company's approach their games this  reaction would help inform how creative  assembly would handle pre-orders and dlc  going forward total war warhammer was  always intended to be a trilogy with the  massive worlds of each game merging  together with the development of each  chapter in the series the titles would  stack on top of each other to create one  seamless and massive world and campaign  this became the mortal empire's campaign  with the october 2017 update however  total war warhammer 2 was focused on the  western new world and its centerpiece  the great vortex this time around there  was much more focus on a narrative  campaign than in previous titles with  the races of the skaven high elves dark  elves and lizard men having their own  unique storylines revolving around  seizing power from the great vortex the  combination of total war warhammer 1 and  2 created the largest total war  experience to date collectively titled  the mortal empires campaign it pulled  together the factions settlements spells  legendary lords and units into the  biggest map the franchise had ever seen  this collection of legendary lords and  factions only grew with the selection of  dlc and free lc that would follow the  release of the game some of which having  their own unique campaigns completely  separate from the struggles of the  vortex with the most interesting  addition in my opinion being that of the  vampire coast interesting because unlike  much of the other factions in the total  war warhammer titles there were no  miniatures to base the unit designs off  of so instead the designers at creative  assembly had to work with games workshop  to use lore snippets of stories and  lists from issues of white dwarf to  create heroes and units to flesh out  their new faction of crustacean riding  zombies and enchanted walking pirate  ships maybe the vampire coast was a  testing ground for this idea as some of  the factions making at the roster for  total war warhammer 3 have been built in  much the same way but between the second  and third entry between the warhammer  titles creative assembly would visit  both history and a different form of  fiction once more total war saga thrones  of britannia was the first game in  creative assembly shorter but more  focused style of campaigns instead of  encompassing a wide era of history the  game revolved around a much shorter time  period thrones of britannia made a much  more detailed britain than we were used  to seeing in historical titles with  players being able to take control of  vikings anglo-saxons as well as the  welsh and gaelic kingdoms to become king  of the isle during the 9th century  series director mike simpson said that  the point of the saga series was to put  a more defined geographical area under a  microscope and build a strong focus on  the culture of the factions lands and  time period this was reflected both in  the gameplay but also on a more micro  level with things like the design of the  ui featuring artwork and unit portraits  that strongly resemble art and  tapestries of the time some think that's  only really doable with a game that  focuses much more deeply on the history  and area that it's representing the game  also offered more courses for victory in  the campaign with conquest fame and  kingdom all culminating into an ultimate  victory but there were also smaller  changes to the gameplay that tied into  the setting for example when recruiting  units they would appear at low strength  and build up which was meant to mimic a  sense of mustering and the game played  more with unique events and branching  dilemmas that were often built around  authentic historical events of the time  period the game did a lot with smaller  elements like this and it felt like a  bed for feature experimentation that  could reappear in the mainline games as  well as being an interesting more  focused take on a smaller piece of  history it was exciting to see as the  more historically focused part of the  fanbase had been waiting a return to the  history books for a number of years the  next game however was an intriguing  blend of historical authenticity and  fictional fantasy  total war three kingdoms shone a  spotlight on the leaders and warlords of  china in the year 220 with a total war  game that was much more character driven  than we were used to seeing the core  inspiration of the game came from the  three kingdoms period of china as well  as the 14th century novelization of the  time period romance of the three  kingdoms the split of two flavors of the  era created two versions of the game the  records version gave players a much more  classical and grounded version of total  war with characters accompanied by  bodyguards the romance version however  played to the larger than life  characters from the novel allowing  one-on-one duels as well as incredible  feats of combat prowess and special  skills the audio of the day however was  still focused on the characters of the  game with more emphasis given to  character development customization and  satisfaction within your faction lords  and generals could create bonds or  rivalries which would affect their  performance in combat if they were  fighting against their sworn enemy or  buffing the morale if they were fighting  side by side with a friend this would  create a lot of interesting moments as  you manage your faction to a higher  degree than just chess pieces on a board  if two characters were bonded and you  executed one of the pair then players  would have to deal with those  repercussions as the campaign progressed  the type of character would also dictate  what units you were able to field in  battle as each champion strategist  vanguard commander or sentinel would be  able to recruit different units and give  those units different advantages for  example strategists were able to field  better range units and vanguards were  best suited to lead the cavalry portion  of your army the emphasis on character  development and their connected gameplay  mechanics as well as building new story  elements proved to be a hit with players  and romance mode was praised for  bringing in some of the action scene in  warhammer titles while also balancing it  with historical authenticity and setting  for this total war 3 kingdoms was able  to set a series record for the most  concurrent players during its week of  release and was at the time the  franchise's fastest selling title  selling a million copies within a week  of its release after toying with pure  history complete fiction and a blend of  both creative assembly's next game to  follow the three kingdoms would bring in  a little bit of the mythological to the  world of total war total war saga troy  is so far the title that has gone  furthest back in history set in the  bronze age of the 13th century bc which  puts it around 900 to a thousand years  before the setting of total war rome  as the name suggests the game is set  during the trojan war but also covers  the surrounding aegean yet it still  retains more of the focused geographical  and cultural scope that the saga series  is built around it's the ancient culture  and subsequent mythology that surrounds  this time period that makes for the  game's unique selling point due to the  lack of historical records and sources  from the location and time period the  design team at creative assembly sophia  instead used the ancient greek epic poem  the iliad to build their world but  instead of ignoring the mythical  elements of the work they instead lent  into creating what was described as the  truth behind the myth with this their  aim was to try and provide historically  authentic explanations behind myths and  legends tying them together with stories  and legends with a modern view for  example centaurs were riders that held  close tied to their horses and rode  without a saddle creating a silhouette  of one form rather than actually being  the half-man half horse that the myth  portrays them as as the game grew  however we saw the release of the mythos  dlc and the ways to play the game grew  to three one being a historical version  of the campaign similar to what the  records mo did for three kingdoms  dialing back the larger than life  legends and giving mighty heroes  retinues of bodyguards the second being  the truth behind the myth where heroes  remain single powerful entities and gods  provided passive buffs with access to  unique interpretations to mythic units  and access to unique interpretations to  mythic units like the minotaur being a  single mighty man wearing the skull of a  bull and thirdly a mythological mode  where legends and myths were more  fleshed out and represented as written  in the iliad heroes grow in power gods  can take more active parts in battles  with poseidon summoning waves against  your enemies the minotaur now being a  legitimate half man half bull and giving  players the ability to bring units like  the fierce and cerberus into their ranks  personally i find the way total war is  experimenting with history and fiction  like they've done with troy and three  kingdoms fascinating and i hope it's one  they continue to toy with originally the  game was a one year epic story exclusive  and was actually a free download as long  as you redeemed it within the first 24  hours of release which caused a surge of  7.5 million copies to be claimed within  a single day  that brings us to the franchise's newest  release and the final entry in the  warhammer trilogy total war warhammer 3.  the focal point of the world is set to  the northern and eastern elements of  warhammer's old world and builds on some  of the more unique factions and  civilizations of the fiction human  factions like kislev and grand cafe were  built in much the same way that the  vampire coasts were in total world war  hammer 2. kislev have very few models to  base designs off of in the case of  cathay there are none at all so instead  creative assembly once again had to draw  inspiration from warhammer's law stories  and these factions real-world  inspirations of medieval russia and  imperial china to help fully realize  these factions in a way that would fit  in the world of total war warhammer and  while there are also the yoga kingdoms  to balance out the roster the focus of  this game is very much the realms of  chaos and demons each chaos god has been  fleshed out to become their own faction  with core nurgle zinc and slaanesh all  having armies of demons led by greater  demons and cultists and each infecting  the world in a different way these  demons can also culminate under one  demon prince allowing players to mix and  match units from different chaotic  factions as one force as the player  fights to earn favor from each chaos god  allowing for different units as well as  a fully customizable faction leader  meaning players can throw on different  wings limbs and adornments depending on  how heavy their worship the campaign map  is also twice the size of that from the  eye of the vortex campaign in total war  warhammer 2. and for the first time  allows players to venture into the  realms of chaos themselves with new  challenges and gameplay elements  appearing in each hellish realm with all  the variety that comes with fully  realizing factions like kislev as well  as featuring some of the more esoteric  ones like nurgle and yoga kingdoms the  game offers some of the franchise's most  diverse flavors and ways to play and  while i'm writing this before the  release of the game we can be confident  this will only grow as the grand  campaign is introduced and more and more  content is brought in through the life  of total war warhammer 3. what's next  for the franchise though is yet to be  seen could creative assembly take on  warhammer's age of sigmar or a new  fictional universe entirely perhaps  they'll revisit medieval with a third  entry into the time period or explore a  new saga altogether only time will tell  but after 22 years 15 main games and a  handful of spin-offs thousands of years  of history covered a 30-year franchise  at their back and nearly every strategy  game player keeping their eyes on them  the history of total war has been a hell  of a lot brighter than the real world  history that they've pulled inspiration  from thanks for watching my history of  the total war franchise a series that  i've adored since i was able to work a  mouse if you want to check out some more  awesome history documentaries we have a  bunch on the channel so be sure to  subscribe for more videos like this and  of course like the video if you enjoyed  it until next time thanks again for  watching and i'll see you in the next  one  you
 

